<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>Voice tools for Tracon. Text-to-speech (TTS) and speech-to-text (STT); generated audio lives in the attachment store. The provider abstraction ships with an ElevenLabs implementation.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(CommonPackageTags);voice;speech;tts;stt;elevenlabs</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Tracon.Core/Tracon.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NO NuGet package is taken on. The surface used is seven HTTP operations in
    four groups (text-to-speech with a streaming and a timestamps variant,
    text-to-dialogue with a timestamps variant, speech-to-text, and the voice
    list) and the JSON contract is simple; raw HttpClient + System.Text.Json
    source generation keeps the package AOT compatible.

    There is no official .NET SDK from ElevenLabs, and the community ones fail
    the target-framework or AOT rule, so the
    contract is a written declaration: tests/Tracon.Voice.UnitTests/Fixtures/
    elevenlabs-operations.json. ElevenLabsOperationDriftTests proves it equals
    what the code does; scripts/elevenlabs-kayma.py checks it against the
    provider's published OpenAPI document.

    A community SDK would carry maintenance and version risk to the consumer
, and measured that an SDK can break SILENTLY at run time
    under centralized version management.
  -->

</Project>
